KG42.register("rules-2fd8fd3c613cf1618b7e9f44",{"id":"04-energy_collectives::v141-c-R013","paper":"04-energy_collectives","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R013","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M02","name":"Local market coordinator","process_stage":"coordination","market_meaning":"Any community-manager decision model g may be implemented subject to the stated convexity restriction. Scope: Choice of community-manager interface decision model. Condition: The decision model must preserve convexity of the whole formulation. The community manager is tasked with preserving fairness inside the collective according to the agreed criteria. Scope: Fairness responsibilities under collective common agreements. Condition: Fairness is interpreted according to the criteria collective members agree to adopt. The community-manager interface objective minimizes day-ahead energy import costs and maximizes day-ahead energy export revenues. Scope: Market-driven community-manager interface example. Condition: When the market-driven interface model is selected. The community-manager interface objective penalizes weighted norms of imported and exported energy to pursue community autonomy. Scope: Community-autonomy interface example. Condition: When the community selects the autonomy objective. The community-manager objective may include a penalty on the maximum prosumer import share. Scope: Maximum-importer penalty interface example. Condition: When the community chooses to penalize the largest individual import contribution. The community manager may coordinate prosumers to provide peak-shaving services by minimizing the maximum imported energy across the considered timestamps. Scope: Multi-temporal peak-shaving interface example. Condition: When a multi-temporal optimization is used to pursue peak shaving. The community-manager interface objective includes geographically differentiated transaction costs on absolute inter-neighbourhood energy flows. Scope: Geographical-preferences interface example with three neighbourhoods. Condition: When the three-neighbourhood geographical-cost model is selected.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function coordination.local_operator under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["coordination.local_operator"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"LEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"coordination","context_text":"Choice of community-manager interface decision model. | Community-autonomy interface example. | Fairness responsibilities under collective common agreements. | Geographical-preferences interface example with three neighbourhoods. | Market-driven community-manager interface example. | Maximum-importer penalty interface example. | Multi-temporal peak-shaving interface example.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19","v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e04"]},"x":[{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","symbol":"q_{imp}","meaning":"Total energy imported by the community from the market and system operator; a global argument of the community-manager interface objective g (e.g., g(q_imp,q_exp) in eq. (4), g(q_imp,q_exp,alpha) in eq. (8), and the T-dimensional vector form in eq. (10)).","kind":"operational","domain":"energy import quantity (real-valued); the supplied evidence does not state a sign or unit restriction; q_imp is linked to the nonnegative prosumer import shares alpha_j via gamma_imp q_imp = gamma_imp sum_j alpha_j (E22) with alpha_j >= 0 (E23).","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","symbol":"q_{exp}","meaning":"Total energy exported by the community to the market and system operator; a global argument of the community-manager interface objective g (eqs. (4), (5)-(6), (8), (10), (12)).","kind":"operational","domain":"energy export quantity (real-valued); the supplied evidence does not state a sign or unit restriction.","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-alpha","symbol":"\\alpha = (\\alpha_1, \\ldots, \\alpha_n)","meaning":"Vector of prosumer import quantities/shares; the maximum-importer interface adds gamma_max ||alpha||_infinity to g (eq. (8)).","kind":"operational","domain":"n-dimensional vector of nonnegative real components alpha_j >= 0, j = 1,...,n (E23: the l-infinity norm when all vector components are non-negative).","unit":null,"indices":["j"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E22","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-theta","symbol":"\\vartheta","meaning":"Auxiliary upper-bound variable (epigraph variable) equal to the l-infinity norm of alpha (eq. (9)) or of the import trajectory q_imp (eq. (11)), introduced to restore decomposability.","kind":"operational","domain":"real-valued upper bound; subject to alpha_j \u003c= vartheta for all j = 1,...,n (eq. (9)) or q_imp,t \u003c= vartheta for all t (eq. (11)).","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e05","v141-b-g02-e07"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-v","symbol":"\\boldsymbol{v} = (v_{12}, v_{13}, v_{23})","meaning":"Energy flows on each line connecting the three neighbourhoods N1, N2, N3; the geographical-preference interface adds gamma_geo^T |v| to g (eq. (12)).","kind":"operational","domain":"three-dimensional vector of real-valued line energy flows v_12, v_13, v_23.","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"variable_id":"v141-c-R013-x-l","symbol":"l","meaning":"Order of the norm ||.||_l used in the community-autonomy interface model, with l = 1,...,infinity; the l1 case gives the linear penalty of eq. (6).","kind":"design_selection","domain":"l in {1, ..., infinity} (norm order choice).","unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-e02"]}],"theta":[{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-lambda-DA","symbol":"\\lambda_{DA}","meaning":"Day-ahead wholesale market price entering the market-based interface objective (4) as the export price and, with tau, the import price.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-tau","symbol":"\\tau","meaning":"Price spread between import and export price; the community buy price is (lambda_DA + tau), the sell price is lambda_DA.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","symbol":"\\gamma_{imp}","meaning":"Penalty factor / price the community is willing to pay (when positive) or receive as compensation (when negative) for importing energy; coefficient of q_imp. Vector of size T in the multi-temporal eqs. (10)-(11).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","symbol":"\\gamma_{exp}","meaning":"Penalty factor / price for exporting energy; coefficient of q_exp. Vector of size T in the multi-temporal eqs. (10)-(11).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e02"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-max","symbol":"\\gamma_{max}","meaning":"Penalization applied to the maximum component of the prosumer import-share vector alpha (the l-infinity norm when all components are nonnegative), enforcing the maximum-importer common agreement (eq. (8)).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-e04","v141-b-g03-E09"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-peak","symbol":"\\gamma_{peak}","meaning":"Penalization coefficient on the maximum imported energy over timestamps in the peak-shaving interface (eq. (10)).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e07"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-geo","symbol":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23})","meaning":"Relative geographical transaction costs associated with the inter-neighbourhood lines; multiplies |v| in the geographical-preference objective (eq. (12)).","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-T","symbol":"T","meaning":"Number of timestamps in the multi-temporal peak-shaving interface; timestamps are indexed t = 1,...,T.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e07"]},{"parameter_id":"v141-c-R013-th-n","symbol":"n","meaning":"Number of prosumers in the collective; alpha = (alpha_1,...,alpha_n) and the max-importer epigraph constraints run j = 1,...,n.","value":{"kind":"unspecified","number_value":null,"text_value":null},"unit":null,"indices":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-e04"]}],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Any community-manager decision model g may be implemented subject to the stated convexity restriction. Scope: Choice of community-manager interface decision model. Condition: The decision model must preserve convexity of the whole formulation.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E19"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C002","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"The community manager is tasked with preserving fairness inside the collective according to the agreed criteria. Scope: Fairness responsibilities under collective common agreements. Condition: Fairness is interpreted according to the criteria collective members agree to adopt.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E28","v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C003","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The community-manager interface objective minimizes day-ahead energy import costs and maximizes day-ahead energy export revenues. Scope: Market-driven community-manager interface example. Condition: When the market-driven interface model is selected.","expression":"g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = (\\lambda_{DA} + \\tau) q_{imp} - \\lambda_{DA} q_{exp} \\quad (4)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-lambda-DA","v141-c-R013-th-tau"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E20","v141-b-g02-e01"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C004","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The community-manager interface objective penalizes weighted norms of imported and exported energy to pursue community autonomy. Scope: Community-autonomy interface example. Condition: When the community selects the autonomy objective.","expression":"g(q_{imp}) = \\gamma_{imp} \\|q_{imp}\\|_l + \\gamma_{exp} \\|q_{exp}\\|_l \\quad (5); \\quad \\text{l1-norm case: } g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp} q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp} q_{exp} \\quad (6)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","v141-c-R013-x-l"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E21","v141-b-g02-e02"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C005","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The community-manager objective may include a penalty on the maximum prosumer import share. Scope: Maximum-importer penalty interface example. Condition: When the community chooses to penalize the largest individual import contribution.","expression":"g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}, \\alpha) = \\gamma_{imp} q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp} q_{exp} + \\gamma_{max} \\|\\alpha\\|_{\\infty} \\quad (8)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","v141-c-R013-x-alpha"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-max"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E23","v141-b-g02-e04"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C006","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The community manager may coordinate prosumers to provide peak-shaving services by minimizing the maximum imported energy across the considered timestamps. Scope: Multi-temporal peak-shaving interface example. Condition: When a multi-temporal optimization is used to pursue peak shaving.","expression":"g(\\mathbf{q}_{imp}, \\mathbf{q}_{exp}) = \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{imp}^{T} \\mathbf{q}_{imp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{exp}^{T} \\mathbf{q}_{exp} + \\gamma_{peak} \\|\\mathbf{q}_{imp}\\|_{\\infty} \\quad (10)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-peak","v141-c-R013-th-T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E26","v141-b-g02-e07"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C007","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The community-manager interface objective includes geographically differentiated transaction costs on absolute inter-neighbourhood energy flows. Scope: Geographical-preferences interface example with three neighbourhoods. Condition: When the three-neighbourhood geographical-cost model is selected.","expression":"g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp} q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp} q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T} |\\boldsymbol{v}| \\quad (12)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","v141-c-R013-x-v"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-geo"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E29","v141-b-g01-E30","v141-b-g02-e09","v141-b-g02-e10"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C008","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The maximum-importer penalty (8) is rewritten in epigraph form using the upper bound vartheta of the l-infinity norm: g(q_imp, q_exp, vartheta) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_max vartheta, with alpha_j \u003c= vartheta for j = 1,...,n.","expression":"g(q_{imp}, q_{exp}, \\vartheta) = \\gamma_{imp} q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp} q_{exp} + \\gamma_{max} \\vartheta \\quad \\text{with} \\quad \\alpha_j \\le \\vartheta, \\; j = 1,\\ldots,n \\quad (9)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","v141-c-R013-x-alpha","v141-c-R013-x-theta"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-max","v141-c-R013-th-n"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E24","v141-b-g02-e05"]},{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R013-C009","kind":"mathematical","role":"supporting","statement":"The peak-shaving interface (10) is linearized by the upper bound vartheta on q_imp,t for all t = 1,...,T: g(q_imp, q_exp, vartheta) = gamma_imp^T q_imp + gamma_exp^T q_exp + gamma_peak vartheta, with q_imp,t \u003c= vartheta for all t.","expression":"g(\\mathbf{q}_{imp}, \\mathbf{q}_{exp}, \\vartheta) = \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{imp}^{T} \\mathbf{q}_{imp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{exp}^{T} \\mathbf{q}_{exp} + \\gamma_{peak} \\vartheta \\quad \\text{with} \\quad q_{imp,t} \\le \\vartheta, \\; \\forall t \\quad (11)","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["coordination.local_operator"],"variable_refs":["v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","v141-c-R013-x-theta"],"parameter_refs":["v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","v141-c-R013-th-gamma-peak","v141-c-R013-th-T"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E27","v141-b-g02-e07"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c1r2__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"as a Stackelberg leader follower game. As this implies an in dependent line of work and a signiﬁcant","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"As already mentioned, we envisage the community manager not only as supervisor of convergence to system optimality but also as interface between collective members and market and system operator. In theory, one can implement any decision model g, as long as it preserves convexity of the whole formulation. We encourage the reader to interpret the interface models proposed below as some of the several possible examples. Different definitions for g are to impact the behavior of its members, as well as their revenues and payments."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":", p y g y dis links to existing work in literature focusing on congestion man- str agement, e.g., of","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In the most simple market-driven case, the community tries to minimize the costs of importing and to maximize the revenues from exporting energy with the day-ahead market. The community interface can be modelled as  g(q_imp, q_exp) = (lambda_DA + tau) q_imp - lambda_DA q_exp   (4)  where lambda_DA is the wholesale market price. Depending on the contingent regulation, the price at which the community could buy energy from the power market is generally higher than the selling one. To consider this possibility, we include a parameter tau describing the spread between import and export price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"selling one. To consider this possibility, we include a parameter τ describing the spread between im","equation_id":"(5), (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"In case the community wants to be as autonomous as possible, one will naturally have  g(q_imp) = gamma_imp ||q_imp||_l + gamma_exp ||q_exp||_l   (5)  where ||.||_l is the l-norm with l = 1, . . . , infinity and gamma_imp and gamma_exp the penalty factors. In case of a l1-norm, (4) becomes  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp   (6)  For gamma_imp = (lambda_DA + tau) and gamma_e = -lambda_DA, (6) generalizes (4), with gamma_imp and gamma_exp representing the price the community is willing to pay (when positive, or to receive as compensation when negative) for exchanging energy with the market or system operator."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"y distributed negotiation mechanisms. For instance, to prevent n- strategic behavior, a community ma","equation_id":"(8)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"For instance, to prevent strategic behavior, a community may choose to penalize the prosumer contributing the most to the import by means of an additional fee. Each member is, therefore, pushed to decrease its import, as this fee increases. We model this by adding a penalization gamma_max to the maximum component of prosumers import shares (corresponding to the l-infinity norm when all vector components are non-negative). The function g then becomes  g(q_imp, q_exp, alpha) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_max ||alpha||_infinity   (8)  where alpha = (alpha_1, . . . , alpha_n). The resulting problem preserves convexity, since the l-infinity norm of a vector is a convex function. However, as the l-infinity norm needs all the components alpha_j to be calculated, the problem cannot be decomposed anymore."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"the maximum imported energy over the considered timestamps (indexed with t = 1, . . . , T ), as in [","equation_id":"(10)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"Minimax principles become useful not only to avoid strategic behaviors but also to implement interesting interface models. For instance, let us assume a multi-temporal optimization for an energy collective. The community manager can coordinate the prosumers to provide peak shaving services by minimizing the maximum imported energy over the considered timestamps (indexed with t = 1, . . . , T), as in [18]. The definition of g becomes  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp^T q_imp + gamma_exp^T q_exp + gamma_peak ||q_imp||_infinity   (10)  where all the bold variables and parameter are to be intended as vectors of size T and gamma_peak the penalization coefficient. Unlike (8), (10) preserves the decomposable structure of the optimization problem."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g(qimp, qexp, ) γimpqimp + γexpqexp + γpeak (A de with qimp,t ≤ϑ , ∀t (11) ex th Fairness of these f","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Fairness of these formulation is granted in terms of minimax principles. The energy 'flows' addressed are flattened more, the higher the penalization coefficient. This does not imply that the system preserves fairness under other definitions, e.g., variation of price or proportional total costs. The system fairness is strictly related to the criteria the collective members agree to adopt."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g p , p g , off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible conﬁg- w uration, but more gene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"Energy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion. We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, v = (v_12, v_13, v_23), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23)."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|  sum_{j in N1} q_j = + v_12 + v_13  sum_{j in N2} q_j = - v_12 + v_23  sum_{j in N3} q_j = - v_13 - v_13   (12)  This formulation allows to represent technical constraints, typical of power flow analysis, in the form of spatial and temporal varying grid tariffs. This becomes fundamental in view of a possible integration of energy collectives in the current system operation. Furthermore, the collective can be fragmented to the extreme, i.e., to single prosumer, accounting for individual trades, as in [19]. In this way, the energy collective becomes a supervised version of a fully peer-to-peer economic dispatch."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c2r3__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"y distributed negotiation mechanisms. For instance, to prevent n- strategic behavior, a community ma","equation_id":"(8), (9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"C. Penalty on the Maximum Importer\n\nCollaborative systems are prone to unfair behaviors, whenever one or more participants behave strategically. The community manager has the task to preserve fairness inside the collective. ... One of the objectives of this work is to demonstrate that fairness among collective participants can be included in the distributed negotiation mechanisms. For instance, to prevent strategic behavior, a community may choose to penalize the prosumer contributing the most to the import by means of an additional fee. Each member is, therefore, pushed to decrease its import, as this fee increases. We model this by adding a penalization \\gamma_{max} to the maximum component of prosumers import shares (corresponding to the l_{\\infty} norm when all vector components are non-negative). The function g then becomes\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}, \\alpha) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\gamma_{max}||\\alpha||_{\\infty}   (8)\n\nwhere \\alpha = (\\alpha_1, . . . , \\alpha_n). The resulting problem preserves convexity, since the l_{\\infty} norm of a vector is a convex function."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E22","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c2r1__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"ESS BASED APPROACH TO FUTURE ELECTRICITY MARKETS 3997 ty to pay (when positive, or to receive as com","equation_id":"(7)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"As we analyze the fairness principles of this formulation, let us consider a situation where the community lacks of generation and faces an import cost gamma_imp q_imp. At optimality, (1d) is satisfied with shadow price equal to gamma_imp. It follows that  gamma_imp q_imp = gamma_imp sum_{j=1}^{n} alpha_j   (7)  Equation (7) shows how proportional fairness is intrinsic in the proposed formulation. The total costs are shared among the players and each prosumer j faces a cost proportional to its contribution alpha_j to the total import."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E24","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"er g(qimp, qexp, ϑ) = γimpqimp + γexpqexp + γmaxϑ with αj ≤ϑ , j = 1, . . . , n (9) In this way, g i","equation_id":"(9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To maintain a decomposable structure, we rewrite (8) as  g(q_imp, q_exp, theta) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_max theta  with  alpha_j \u003c= theta , j = 1, . . . , n   (9)  In this way, g is expressed as function of the upper bound, theta, of the l-infinity norm, also known as epigraph formulation, and the inequality constraints grant to achieve the same solution and to allow for decomposition techniques."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E27","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r3__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g(qimp, qexp, ) γimpqimp + γexpqexp + γpeak (A de with qimp,t ≤ϑ , ∀t (11) ex th Fairness of these f","equation_id":"(11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"To simplify the problem solution, the l-infinity norm can still be linearized by means of the upper bound of q_imp,t for all t = 1, . . . , T. It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp, theta) = gamma_imp^T q_imp + gamma_exp^T q_exp + gamma_peak theta  with  q_imp,t \u003c= theta , for all t   (11)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e01","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c1r3__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":", p y g y dis links to existing work in literature focusing on congestion man- str agement, e.g., of","equation_id":"(4)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"A. Market-Based Interface\n\nIn the most simple market-driven case, the community tries to minimize the costs of importing and to maximize the revenues from exporting energy with the day-ahead market. The community interface can be modelled as\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = (\\lambda_{DA} + \\tau)q_{imp} - \\lambda_{DA}q_{exp}   (4)\n\nwhere \\lambda_{DA} is the wholesale market price. Depending on the contingent regulation, the price at which the community could buy energy from the power market is generally higher than the selling one. To consider this possibility, we include a parameter \\tau describing the spread between import and export price."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e02","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c1r4__v141-a-M02-pricing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"selling one. To consider this possibility, we include a parameter τ describing the spread between im","equation_id":"(5), (6)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"B. Community Autonomy\n\nIn case the community wants to be as autonomous as possible, one will naturally have\n\ng(q_{imp}) = \\gamma_{imp}||q_{imp}||_l + \\gamma_{exp}||q_{exp}||_l           (5)\n\nwhere ||\\cdot||_l is the l-norm with l = 1, . . . , \\infty and \\gamma_{imp} and \\gamma_{exp} the penalty factors. In case of a l1-norm, (4) becomes\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp}            (6)\n\nFor \\gamma_{imp} = (\\lambda_{DA} + \\tau) and \\gamma_e = -\\lambda_{DA}, (6) generalizes (4), with \\gamma_{imp} and \\gamma_{exp} representing the price the community is willing"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e05","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p04-c2r4__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":4,"printed_page":"3997","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"er g(qimp, qexp, ϑ) = γimpqimp + γexpqexp + γmaxϑ with αj ≤ϑ , j = 1, . . . , n (9) In this way, g i","equation_id":"(9)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"However, as the l_{\\infty} norm needs all the components \\alpha_j to be calculated, the problem cannot be decomposed anymore. To maintain a decomposable structure, we rewrite (8) as\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}, \\vartheta) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\gamma_{max}\\vartheta\n\nwith         \\alpha_j \\le \\vartheta ,     j = 1, . . . , n       (9)\n\nIn this way, g is expressed as function of the upper bound, \\vartheta, of the l_{\\infty} norm, also known as epigraph formulation, and the inequality constraints grant to achieve the same solution and to allow for decomposition techniques."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e07","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r2__v141-a-M02-clearing","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"the maximum imported energy over the considered timestamps (indexed with t = 1, . . . , T ), as in [","equation_id":"(10), (11)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"the maximum imported energy over the considered timestamps (indexed with t = 1, . . . , T ), as in [18]. The definition of g becomes\n\ng(\\mathbf{q}_{imp}, \\mathbf{q}_{exp}) = \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{imp}^{T}\\mathbf{q}_{imp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{exp}^{T}\\mathbf{q}_{exp} + \\gamma_{peak}||\\mathbf{q}_{imp}||_{\\infty}   (10)\n\nwhere all the bold variables and parameter are to be intended as vectors of size T and \\gamma_{peak} the penalization coefficient. Unlike (8), (10) preserves the decomposable structure of the optimization problem. To simplify the problem solution, the l_{\\infty} norm can still be linearized by means of the upper bound of q_{imp,t} for all t = 1, . . . , T. It follows that\n\ng(\\mathbf{q}_{imp}, \\mathbf{q}_{exp}, \\vartheta) = \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{imp}^{T}\\mathbf{q}_{imp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{exp}^{T}\\mathbf{q}_{exp} + \\gamma_{peak}\\vartheta\n\nwith          q_{imp,t} \\le \\vartheta ,     \\forall t            (11)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e09","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c1r4__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"g p , p g , off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible conﬁg- w uration, but more gene","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 2","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"E. Geographical Preferences\n\nEnergy collectives are to be envisaged as a market construct and not related to a certain positioning on the electric power grid. In practice, this means that operation on a micro-grid, both off- and connected to the grid [6], is only one possible configuration, but more generally such communities may be formed irrespective of grid connection and geographical location. The use of distribution (or transmission for longer distances) networks for community trades results in additional grid costs including several parameters, e.g. geographical distance and lines congestion.\nWe include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N_{1}, N_{2}, N_{3}. We model this system by splitting (1c) in three sub-communities and by defining energy flows, \\boldsymbol{v} = (v_{12}, v_{13}, v_{23}), on each line connecting the neighbourhoods, as depicted in Fig. 2. We include geographical differentiation as relative transaction costs"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e10","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p05-c2r1__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":5,"printed_page":"3998","section":"III. The Role of Community Manager","anchor_text":"IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON POWER SYSTEMS, VOL. 34, NO. 5, SEPTEMBER 2019 γgeo = (γgeo,12, γgeo,13, γgeo,23","equation_id":"(12)","figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-05.png","occurrence_role":"formula"},"source_text":"\\gamma_{geo} = (\\gamma_{geo,12}, \\gamma_{geo,13}, \\gamma_{geo,23}). It follows that\n\ng(q_{imp}, q_{exp}) = \\gamma_{imp}q_{imp} + \\gamma_{exp}q_{exp} + \\boldsymbol{\\gamma}_{geo}^{T}|\\boldsymbol{v}|\n\n\\sum_{j \\in N_1} q_j = + v_{12} + v_{13}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_2} q_j = - v_{12} + v_{23}\n\\sum_{j \\in N_3} q_j = - v_{13} - v_{13}                    (12)"},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g03-E09","location":{"location_id":"src-dfa28329fc-p09-c1r3__v141-a-M02-coordination","page":9,"printed_page":"4002","section":"V. Application and Case-Studies","anchor_text":"are almost always equal among prosumers (standard deviation w of 0.02). This implies that also prosu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 5","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-09.png","occurrence_role":"condition"},"source_text":"The results so far discussed consider a fixed penalization coefficient on the maximum importer of γ_max = 1 $/MWh. As displayed in Fig. 5, where γ_max varies from 0 $/MWh, as in the market-based community trading case, and 1 $/MWh, the impact of common agreements on the investigated fairness indicators changes for different penalty factors."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":null,"empty_theta_reason":null},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M01-coordination","v141-a-M02-clearing","v141-a-M02-coordination","v141-a-M02-pricing"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"coordination.local_operator","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E19","quote":"As already mentioned, we envisage the community manager not only as supervisor of convergence to system optimality but also as interface between collective members and market and system operator. In theory, one can implement any decision model g, as long as it preserves convexity of the whole formulation."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g02-e04","quote":"The community manager has the task to preserve fairness inside the collective."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E28","quote":"The system fairness is strictly related to the criteria the collective members agree to adopt."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E20","quote":"In the most simple market-driven case, the community tries to minimize the costs of importing and to maximize the revenues from exporting energy with the day-ahead market. The community interface can be modelled as  g(q_imp, q_exp) = (lambda_DA + tau) q_imp - lambda_DA q_exp   (4)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E21","quote":"In case the community wants to be as autonomous as possible, one will naturally have  g(q_imp) = gamma_imp ||q_imp||_l + gamma_exp ||q_exp||_l   (5)  where ||.||_l is the l-norm with l = 1, . . . , infinity and gamma_imp and gamma_exp the penalty factors."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E23","quote":"We model this by adding a penalization gamma_max to the maximum component of prosumers import shares (corresponding to the l-infinity norm when all vector components are non-negative). The function g then becomes  g(q_imp, q_exp, alpha) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_max ||alpha||_infinity   (8)"}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E26","quote":"The community manager can coordinate the prosumers to provide peak shaving services by minimizing the maximum imported energy over the considered timestamps (indexed with t = 1, . . . , T), as in [18]."}],[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E29","quote":"We include these additional costs by investigating a setup where an energy collective is formed by prosumers from three different neighbourhoods, N1, N2, N3."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E30","quote":"gamma_geo = (gamma_geo,12, gamma_geo,13, gamma_geo,23). It follows that  g(q_imp, q_exp) = gamma_imp q_imp + gamma_exp q_exp + gamma_geo^T |v|"}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"fd7fc5ab17f42b6e6d58336321d3188c1ee875a1279f16e0b1bf1badda0a3318","applicability":"formalized","reason":"The frozen rule node coordination.local_operator is the community-manager interface decision model g. Unreviewed B evidence supplies explicit source formulas for the interface variants: eq. (4) market-based, eq. (5)-(6) community autonomy, eq. (8) maximum-importer penalty, eq. (10) peak shaving, eq. (12) geographical preferences, plus the epigraph reformulations eqs. (9) and (11). C003-C007 are therefore formalized with formalization_origin=source, and C008-C009 are added as supporting source-supported reformulations. C001 (convexity admissibility) and C002 (fairness criteria) remain procedural/not_formalized because no source formula expresses them. Variables q_imp, q_exp, alpha, vartheta, v and l, and parameters lambda_DA, tau, gamma_imp, gamma_exp, gamma_max, gamma_peak, gamma_geo, T and n are bound to coordination.local_operator as the arguments and coefficients of g. B evidence remains unreviewed; source ambiguities and missing domain/unit details are flagged in revision_requests.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-q-imp","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"q_imp is a global argument of the community-manager interface objective g, the frozen node coordination.local_operator; source eqs. (4), (8), (10), (12) define g as a function of q_imp."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-q-exp","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"q_exp is a global argument of the community-manager interface objective g, the frozen node coordination.local_operator; source eqs. (4), (5)-(6), (8), (10), (12) define g as a function of q_exp."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-alpha","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"alpha is the prosumer import-share vector whose l-infinity norm is penalized inside g (eq. (8)); it enters the coordination.local_operator objective as an argument."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-theta","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"vartheta is the epigraph upper bound used to express the l-infinity norm terms of g in eqs. (9) and (11); it is an auxiliary variable of the coordination.local_operator objective."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-v","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"v is the vector of inter-neighbourhood flows whose absolute value enters the geographical-preference term gamma_geo^T |v| of g (eq. (12))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-x-l","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"l selects the norm order ||.||_l inside the community-autonomy form of g (eqs. (5)-(6)); it is a design selection of the coordination.local_operator interface."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-lambda-DA","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"lambda_DA is the day-ahead wholesale price coefficient in the market-based form of g (eq. (4))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-tau","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"tau is the import/export price spread coefficient in the market-based form of g (eq. (4))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-imp","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"gamma_imp is the import penalty/price coefficient appearing in the interface objective g in eqs. (4), (6), (8), (10) and (12)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-exp","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"gamma_exp is the export penalty/price coefficient appearing in the interface objective g in eqs. (5)-(6), (8), (10) and (12)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-max","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"gamma_max is the coefficient of the maximum-importer penalty ||alpha||_infinity in g (eq. (8)) and of its epigraph variable vartheta (eq. (9))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-peak","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"gamma_peak is the coefficient of the peak-shaving penalty ||q_imp||_infinity in g (eq. (10)) and of its epigraph variable vartheta (eq. (11))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-gamma-geo","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"gamma_geo is the geographical transaction-cost vector multiplying |v| in g (eq. (12))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-T","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"T is the timestamp horizon size dimensioning the vector variables and parameters of the peak-shaving form of g (eqs. (10)-(11))."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-th-n","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"n is the number of prosumers, dimensioning alpha and the epigraph constraints of g in eqs. (8)-(9)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C001","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C001 is the admissibility condition on the coordination.local_operator decision model g (convexity preservation)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C002","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C002 assigns the fairness-preservation task to the coordination.local_operator under collectively agreed criteria."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C003","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C003 formalizes the market-based interface objective g, which is the frozen node coordination.local_operator, using eq. (4)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C004","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C004 formalizes the community-autonomy interface objective g, which is the frozen node coordination.local_operator, using eqs. (5)-(6)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C005","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C005 formalizes the maximum-importer penalty interface objective g, which is the frozen node coordination.local_operator, using eq. (8)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C006","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C006 formalizes the peak-shaving interface objective g, which is the frozen node coordination.local_operator, using eq. (10)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C007","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C007 formalizes the geographical-preference interface objective g, which is the frozen node coordination.local_operator, using eq. (12)."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C008","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C008 formalizes the epigraph reformulation (9) of the maximum-importer penalty inside the coordination.local_operator objective g."},{"object_id":"v141-c-R013-C009","node_ids":["coordination.local_operator"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"C009 formalizes the epigraph linearization (11) of the peak-shaving penalty inside the coordination.local_operator objective g."}],"revision_requests":["R013-C004: eq. (5) is printed as g(q_imp) = gamma_imp||q_imp||_l + gamma_exp||q_exp||_l, with a left-hand side listing only q_imp although the right-hand side also contains q_exp. B re-extraction should confirm whether the intended statement is g(q_imp, q_exp).","R013-C004: the text 'for gamma_imp = (lambda_DA + tau) and gamma_e = -lambda_DA' uses gamma_e while gamma_exp is used elsewhere; verify the printed subscript.","R013-C007: eq. (12) third sub-community balance is printed as sum_{j in N3} q_j = -v_13 - v_13; the repeated v_13 is unresolved (possibly -v_23). B re-extraction should verify the printed equation.","Variable domains and units are not explicitly given in the supplied evidence catalog for q_imp, q_exp and the gamma parameters; C.1 should attach the nomenclature and eq. (1) evidence locations (e.g., 'q_imp and q_exp are continuous variables', alpha_j/beta_j definitions, and the $/MWh calibration) to make domains and units source-verified.","Eqs. (10)-(11) use bold T-dimensional vectors for q_imp, q_exp and gamma_imp/gamma_exp while eqs. (4)-(9) use them as scalars; C.1 should confirm whether the frozen representation should distinguish scalar and vector symbol objects for R013.","E19 states that different definitions of g impact members' revenues and payments, but frozen R013 has E=[]; C.1 should confirm whether R013 needs an edge from coordination.local_operator to settlement.payment/clearing.local or whether that relationship is intentionally owned by R010/R011/R012."]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
